window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][102] = "<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>(The candidate has been told the team works on shared photo libraries; the details are invented.)</em> &quot;I've used shared photo libraries with my family for a year, and what I noticed is that the hard part isn't sharing, it's the moment after: a thousand photos arrive and no one knows which ones are meant for them. In my last role I rebuilt a gallery flow and raised weekly active use by 11% by showing fewer, better-chosen items first, so the problem of selection over quantity is one I've worked on. I read what the team has said publicly about the feature and looked at the release notes, and my honest critique is that the 'who is in this photo' suggestion works well for adults and is unreliable for children's changing faces, which I think matters for families; I'd want to understand how the team weighs the privacy of on-device recognition against accuracy. I don't know the constraints, so I'd rather ask than assume. Why now: I want to work where the details of a product are treated as the product, and where privacy is a design decision. I'm curious how the team decides what to leave out.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A specific observation; a link to a past result; a critique with an honest limit; craft and privacy awareness; a question.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would you change about the product, and what would it cost? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd test an option that groups a burst of shared photos by event and shows the three most representative first. The cost is engineering effort and the risk of hiding a photo someone wanted, so I'd measure whether people open the full set (a guardrail) and how many photos each recipient views. I'd start with an opt-in group.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> You may find we can't tell you much about the work. How do you feel about that? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I understand it, and I respect it. I'd rather join knowing what I can about the team and its principles, and trust that the specifics come with the role. I won't share details of my current employer's unreleased work either.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-02 · Beginner · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you research a company before an interview?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I read the website and look at recent news.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses the product, reads primary sources and forms a view.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Builds a one-page brief in about 90 minutes (products and users, strategy, metrics, competitors, recent moves, my angle, three questions), separates primary sources from inference, and uses it across the loop.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I build a one-page company brief, in about 90 minutes. First I use the product for a week, or at least thirty minutes of real tasks, and write down what I notice. Then I read primary sources: the newsroom, the annual report or investor letter for a public company, the product blog, release notes, help-center pages, and talks by the team's leaders. I write down how the company makes money, its stated priorities and the numbers it reports. I pick two or three competitors and say where the company is strong and weak. I list the last two or three launches or changes.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then comes the part most people skip: my angle. One observation about the product, one critique with an improvement and its cost, and what I'd examine first in the role. I add three questions that my reading raised. I mark every claim as primary or inference, and I leave out anything I can't source. I use the brief for 'why us,' for product questions, and for the questions at the end.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Method; primary sources; a point of view; honesty about sources.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the company is secretive and publishes little? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd rely on the product itself, the public statements and talks of its leaders, release notes and support documentation, and say plainly what I can't know. For a team-specific loop I'd read what the team has published and ask the recruiter what to review.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you avoid just repeating press coverage? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By testing the product myself and forming a critique; press is secondary. If I mention a fact from an article, I label it that way and I check whether the company confirms it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-03 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A team wants to add a new capability. How do you decide whether it belongs in the operating system or in a separate app?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Put it in the OS because it's more integrated,&quot; or &quot;an app because it's faster.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names criteria such as reach, privacy, discoverability and iteration speed.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Weighs the criteria with numbers, considers the developer ecosystem and maintenance, proposes a hybrid, names what would change the decision, and considers users with one device and different needs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd decide with criteria, not instinct. The criteria that usually matter: <em>system-wide reach</em> (does the capability need to work inside many other apps?); <em>privacy and trust</em> (does it need system-level access that an app should not have, or processing that should stay on the device?); <em>discoverability</em> (will people find it where they need it?); <em>speed of iteration</em> (apps can update independently; an OS release is slow and carries more risk); and <em>maintenance cost</em> (a system feature must work for years and across devices).</p>\n<p>&quot;Suppose I weight these 30%, 25%, 15%, 15% and 15%, and score each option from one to five. As an OS feature: reach 5, privacy 4, discoverability 4, iteration 2, maintenance 2: a weighted score of 3.70. As a separate app: 2, 3, 3, 5, 4: 3.15. As a hybrid, a small system layer providing the capability to apps through an interface, with apps shaping the experience: 4, 4, 4, 4, 3: 3.85. The scores are judgments, and the point is to make them visible and challengeable.</p>\n<p>&quot;So I'd lean to the hybrid: build the system layer only for what must be system-wide (access, privacy, consistency), and let apps, including our own, build experiences on top, so we can iterate faster and let developers extend it. I would also test the choice for a person who owns only one device and for people with different needs, such as accessibility settings: a system layer reaches them by default, while an app reaches only those who find and install it. What would change my mind: evidence that the capability is rarely used inside other apps (then an app is enough), or a privacy requirement that makes anything but a system implementation unsafe (then OS).&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Criteria with weights; a hybrid; ecosystem and privacy thinking; stated reversal conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you validate the weights? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd test how sensitive the answer is: scaling the other weights proportionally, the OS option overtakes the hybrid only if reach is weighted about 40% or more, so the hybrid holds across a wide range of weights. Then I'd check the scores with engineering (maintenance, iteration) and design (discoverability), since those are their judgments, not mine.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What about users who don't update the OS promptly? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;That is a reason to keep experience-level changes in the app layer, which updates faster, and to design the system layer to be stable and backward compatible. I'd look at the update adoption by version to see how many users are on older releases.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-04 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a feature that works better across a person's devices without penalizing people who own only one.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Sync everything between devices.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a baseline for single-device users and an enhancement for multi-device users.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats the single-device experience as complete, makes the multi-device benefit additive and optional, handles privacy and security of cross-device data, measures both groups, and considers failure and setup.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Principle: the single-device experience is complete, and the second device makes it better, never the other way round. Take the example of a feature for handing off a task, say continuing a document or a call on another device. The baseline: on one device, the feature works fully, and nothing nags the person to buy another. The enhancement: when a second device is nearby and signed in to the same account, the task offers to continue there, with no setup beyond signing in.</p>\n<p>&quot;Design details. <em>Discovery:</em> show the enhancement only when it is relevant (a second device is present), never as an advertisement. <em>Privacy:</em> data moves between the person's devices with end-to-end protection, processed on the device where possible, and the person can turn it off per device. <em>Failure:</em> if the second device is off or out of range, the first continues without error. <em>Setup:</em> automatic when the account matches; no pairing codes where avoidable. <em>Accessibility:</em> the handoff works with assistive features on both devices.</p>\n<p>&quot;Measurement. For multi-device users: the share who use the handoff weekly, time to resume a task and satisfaction. For single-device users, the guardrail: the feature's completion rate and satisfaction must not be lower than before, and they must not report being nagged. Suppose (invented) 60% of active users have two or more devices; the benefit is focused on 60%, and the risk is a worse experience for 40%. I'd test with a holdout, and read the single-device guardrail by week.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A complete baseline; an additive enhancement; privacy and failure design; two-group measurement.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Isn't a nudge to buy more devices good for the business? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It may be, and I'd keep the product experience honest: the feature should be valuable for what it does. A nudge that people experience as pressure would hurt trust, and I'd measure complaints and opt-outs. If leadership wants a purchase path, I'd put it where a person is already looking at devices, not inside the task.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the cross-device sync has a security flaw? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Design for it up front: minimal data, end-to-end protection, a kill switch per feature, staged rollout and an incident plan (C25.4). A convenience feature that exposes data would cost more than it earned.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-05 · Hard · interview-craft · IC · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A team wants to log more usage data to improve a feature. How do you decide?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;More data is better for improving the product, so log it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks what question the data answers and considers privacy.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Applies necessity, alternatives (on-device processing, aggregation, sampling), consent, retention and access; does sample-size arithmetic to show how much data is needed; sets a decision rule and a review.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I'm describing principles Apple has said it applies publicly, such as on-device processing and privacy-preserving techniques, and nothing about any real system <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple privacy pages]</code>. &quot;I'd start by asking what decision the data will inform. If the team can't state the question and the metric, the answer is no. Then I'd work down a ladder: (1) <em>Do we need it at all?</em> Could a user study or existing data answer the question? (2) <em>Can it stay on the device?</em> Compute the measure locally and send only the result. (3) <em>Can it be aggregated or noised?</em> Send counts, not events, and use privacy-preserving techniques so that individual behavior cannot be recovered. (4) <em>If it must be collected,</em> collect the minimum, with consent, a short retention period, restricted access and a plan for deletion.</p>\n<p>&quot;The arithmetic helps decide how much is really needed. To detect a one-point change in completion from a 50% baseline with 95% confidence and 80% power, an experiment needs about 2 × (1.96 + 0.84)² × 0.25 ÷ 0.01² = 39,200 users per group. If only 20% of eligible users opt in to share aggregate data, that's about 196,000 eligible users per group, which is small for a feature used by millions. So we can answer the question with aggregated, opt-in measurement; we do not need detailed event logs from everyone.</p>\n<p>&quot;The decision: approve a narrowly scoped, aggregated, opt-in measurement with a two-year review, and decline the raw event log. What would change my mind: a quality problem that aggregation can't diagnose (for example, rare failures), in which case I'd propose a time-limited, consented diagnostic program for a small group, with deletion at the end.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A decision question; a ladder of less intrusive options; sample-size reasoning; a decision with conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The team says they can't debug rare failures without event data. What then? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd offer a diagnostic mode a user can choose to turn on and send when a problem happens, with clear text about what is sent, automatic deletion afterward, and redaction of identifiers. That gives engineers examples of failures without collecting everyone's behavior.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle a competitor that collects more and iterates faster? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd compare the actual speed difference: often the constraint is the quality of the question, not the volume of data. If it is a real disadvantage, I'd say so to leadership with numbers and look for privacy-preserving ways to close it, and I'd treat trust as a long-term advantage I would not trade for a short-term one.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-06 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you decide what to leave out of a new feature?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I cut anything that's not essential.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Anchors on the user's core job and removes what does not serve it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines the job, tests each element by removing it, uses usage evidence, protects against hidden costs of complexity, and handles the pressure to add options and settings.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I start with the job the feature does for the person, in one sentence: for example, 'set up a new device in under two minutes without reading anything.' Then each element has to earn its place: if I remove it, does the job still get done? I'd test that in a prototype with users: take away one thing at a time and watch for confusion. Suppose a settings screen has 12 options, and usage data show the top three account for 91% of changes. The other nine can be defaults with sensible behavior, or moved into an advanced area, because each visible option has a cost: it must be understood, tested, translated and supported, and it makes the common path less clear.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd also ask what the omitted thing costs the people who need it: for some options (accessibility settings, say), the usage share is low and the importance is high, so usage alone is not the criterion. I'd keep those and make them easy to find. When stakeholders ask for more options, I'd ask what problem each solves and whether a better default solves it without a choice. And I'd write down what we left out and why, so that the decision can be revisited with evidence instead of being relitigated by whoever shouts loudest.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The job as the test; removal tests; evidence plus judgment; protection of high-importance, low-usage needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> A senior stakeholder insists on adding an option. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask what situation it serves and how many people are in it, and propose a test: ship without it and measure the support contacts and drop-off the stakeholder predicts. If the evidence shows a real cost, we add it. If they still insist and the decision is theirs, I'd commit and keep the reversal condition in writing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tell something is essential when users ask for it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I look at what they are trying to achieve, not just what they ask for. If many ask for the same option but the underlying job can be met by a better default, I fix the default.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-07 · Hard · interview-craft · IC · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you prioritize accessibility features against features for the mainstream?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Accessibility affects fewer users, so mainstream features come first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats accessibility as a requirement and a benefit for all.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Rejects the framing as a trade-off, sizes the need, uses the benefit to everyone, sets a floor, involves users with disabilities, measures it, and handles cost honestly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd challenge the framing. Accessibility isn't a competitor to mainstream features; it is a quality bar for every feature, and some accessibility work is also a mainstream feature. A widely cited estimate from the World Health Organization is that about 1.3 billion people, around one in six, experience significant disability <code>Public</code> <code>[V: WHO fact sheet on disability]</code>, so it is not a small segment, and many more have temporary or situational limits (a broken arm, a loud room, bright sun).</p>\n<p>&quot;So I'd set a floor: a feature does not ship if it fails the accessibility requirements for its type (screen reader support, text scaling, contrast, captions, motor alternatives). That is part of the definition of done (C16.8), not a separate prioritization. Beyond the floor, dedicated accessibility features are prioritized on need, with people with disabilities in the research and the design, not for them. Look for features that serve both: captions help the deaf and anyone in a noisy place; voice control helps people with motor limits and anyone with busy hands.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd measure it: the share of features meeting the bar at launch, task success for users of assistive technology in testing, adoption of the features, and satisfaction in that group, with the legal and ethical requirements as a floor. Cost is real, and cheaper at design time than later; I'd say so to stakeholders with an example. I would not defend it as a cost-benefit calculation alone; it is also a matter of who the product is for.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Reframes the question; sizes the need; a floor in the definition of done; co-design; honest about cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> A launch will slip two weeks to fix accessibility issues. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;If the issues block a group of users from the core task, I'd recommend the delay or a staged launch that holds back the affected path until it is fixed, and give leadership the cost and the risk of shipping a product some people can't use. If the issues are minor, I'd ship with a dated fix, and document it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you get a reliable view from users with disabilities? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By recruiting them as research participants and design partners early, paying them for their time, testing real tasks with their own assistive technology, and acting on what we hear. I'd also use established guidelines as a baseline and treat them as a minimum.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-08 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you improve how families share and organize photos together?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add more sharing options and albums.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments users, picks a problem and proposes a solution with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes roles (parent, child, grandparent), identifies the problem of selection and consent, considers privacy for children, proposes a test, names tradeoffs, and measures contribution and engagement across the group.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd start with who is in a family library: the parent who organizes, the other parent who contributes, a teenager who wants privacy, and a grandparent who just wants to see the grandchildren. Their jobs differ. The organizer wants a complete, tidy record; contributors want sharing to be effortless; the teenager wants control over what is visible; the grandparent wants a simple way to see the best of recent photos without sorting through thousands.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd pick the problem that seems largest: relatives drowning in volume and not seeing the moments that matter. Evidence I'd look for: the share of shared photos that are ever viewed by the other members; the share of members who stop opening the library after the first month; and interviews with grandparents. Suppose (invented) only 12% of shared photos are viewed by a second member and a third of non-organizers stop within a month.</p>\n<p>&quot;The solution: a 'highlights' view for each event that shows the three or four most representative photos (selected by quality and diversity of people and moments), with the full set one tap away, and a weekly digest for members who want it. Tradeoffs: automatic selection can hide a photo someone cares about, so I'd always make the full set available and let anyone flag a missed photo; selection of images of children raises privacy concerns, so processing stays on the device where possible and the organizer controls who sees what.</p>\n<p>&quot;Metrics: the share of shared photos viewed by a second member (primary), members active at 90 days, and contributions per member; guardrails: complaints, flagged misses and opt-outs. I'd test with a holdout of families for four weeks and read by member role, since the benefit for grandparents may come at a cost to organizers if they feel they lose control.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Roles and jobs; a specific problem with evidence; privacy and consent; role-level measurement.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> A teenager doesn't want their photos in the family highlights. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Give each member control over which of their photos are eligible and offer a default that respects age: for teenagers, an opt-in. A feature that overrides a teenager's choice would hurt trust, and I'd measure complaints from that group directly.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you know the highlight selection is good? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd have members rate a sample of selections, measure whether they open the full set (a sign of missing something), count the photos flagged as missed, and compare views per photo against the old experience. A good selection raises views of the chosen photos without raising 'what happened to my photo' complaints.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-09 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What questions would you ask your interviewers?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;What's the culture like? What do you like about working here?&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tailors questions to the interviewer and role.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Asks questions that show preparation and judgment, listens, uses an answer in a follow-up, and avoids questions answered on the careers page or about pay in early rounds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd tailor two or three questions to the person. To the hiring manager: 'What would success look like in the first six months, and what is the hardest problem the team is working on where you'd want help?' To a peer PM: 'Tell me about a decision you changed your mind about recently and why.' To an engineer: 'What makes a PM easy to work with from your side, and what makes one hard?' To a senior leader: 'What do you think will matter most for this product in the next two years?' To the recruiter: 'What does the rest of the process look like, and what do you see in the strongest candidates?'</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd listen and use the answer. If the manager says the hardest problem is onboarding for new users, I'd say, 'In my last role we found the first-day drop-off was in the permission step; is that where you see the loss?' That shows I was listening. I'd avoid questions I can answer from the careers page, questions about pay or vacation with the interviewers (save them for the recruiter), and empty flattery. I'd keep to two or three, so that I don't run over time, and I'd note the answers for the follow-up message.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Tailoring; curiosity; listening; judgment about what to ask whom.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the interviewer already answered your questions during the interview? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd say so and ask a deeper one: 'You mentioned the team is shifting focus to new markets; what is the biggest thing you've learned so far from that?' A prepared list is a start, and listening produces better questions.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Is it acceptable to ask about concerns, such as whether the team has had turnover? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Yes, if it is asked respectfully and specifically: 'How has the team changed over the past year, and what did you learn from it?' It shows I'm thinking about whether the role is right for me, which is a fair thing to do.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-10\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-10 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You receive a 48-hour take-home product case. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd spend the 48 hours making it as good as possible.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Restates the question, plans the structure and edits.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Time-boxes to the stated expectation, answers first, includes one or two numbers and a segment insight, states assumptions, recommends with risks and a test plan, and cuts the length.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd treat the 48 hours as a limit and not a target. If the company says it should take about eight hours, I'd spend about eight. A sprawling submission suggests poor judgment, and the reader's time is limited.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd restate the question and the audience in two sentences, then plan the structure: page one has the recommendation and the key number; page two the user, the problem and the evidence; page three the options and tradeoffs; page four the test plan, the decision rule and the risks. I'd budget the time: two hours to think and gather facts, three to write, one for numbers, one to edit, one buffer.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd show one or two numbers with the logic, and look for a segment insight that an average would hide. For a subscription proposal, for example, the average member may be profitable while the heaviest users are not. I'd state my assumptions, make a clear recommendation, and say what I would do with more time. Then I'd edit: cut a third, move the answer to the top, check every number, and read the prompt again to make sure I answered what was asked. I'd send it early and keep a record of my assumptions in case there is a follow-up conversation.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Respect for the time limit; answer first; numbers and a segment insight; editing; a plan to defend it.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the prompt is ambiguous and you can't ask? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd state my interpretation at the top and my assumptions, and note how the recommendation would change under the other reading. If I can ask the recruiter, I'd ask one specific clarifying question.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you decide what to cut? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;What doesn't change the recommendation or its credibility: background the reader knows, a long list of options I won't recommend (I keep one line each), and detail in the appendix instead of the body. If a paragraph does not help the reader decide, it goes.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c33-11\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C33-11 · Hard · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You have loops at two companies in the same month, one built on stories and a written exercise, the other on product sense and analytical rounds. How do you prepare?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd study as much as I can for both.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the differences and allocates time.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates shared from specific preparation with hours, confirms formats, allocates by importance times gap, schedules the loops, plans mocks, and keeps policies and briefs separate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;First I'd confirm each format with the recruiter: round types, durations, tools, decider and the policy on assistance. Then I'd split the work into what is shared and what is specific. Shared: the story bank with probes, metrics fundamentals, structure and communication, product sense basics. Specific: for the stories-and-written-exercise company, written practice and probe depth; for the other, timed product and analytical rounds in 45-minute format and committee-style evidence.</p>\n<p>&quot;With about 50 hours each if prepared separately, 100 in all, suppose the shared work is 24 hours and each specific part 26: 24 + 26 + 26 = 76 hours, a saving of a quarter. I'd allocate within each part by importance times gap: after a mock for each, I'd rate my strength by round type and put hours where importance is high and strength low (as in Worked example 1).</p>\n<p>&quot;The schedule: weeks one and two, the shared work and two mocks; week three, the first company's specific work and its loop; week four, the second company's specific work and its loop. I'd write a separate one-page company brief for each and keep their policies straight (one may prohibit assistance explicitly). After the first loop I'd write down what I was asked, re-score and adjust the second plan. I'd stop new material two days before each loop, and plan logistics, including time zones, for both.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Format first; shared versus specific with numbers; weights; schedule; learning between loops.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the loops are on the same week? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask one recruiter whether an earlier or later date is possible; most will accommodate a request made early. If not, I'd put the shared work first and focus the last days on the company whose format is the less familiar to me, and protect sleep, since fatigue costs more than another hour of study.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Is it worth preparing for the one you want less? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Yes, since an offer is an option, and since practice transfers. I'd keep the time proportional to my interest only after checking that I don't want it less because I'm afraid of it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c33-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented, and the companies and schedules are fictional.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C33-01 · Allocate your hours</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You have 60 hours before a loop of four rounds: product design (importance 3, your strength 3), analytics (importance 3, strength 2), technical (importance 2, strength 2) and behavioral (importance 3, strength 4). Compute hours by round type using importance times gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Loop preparation.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Gap = 5 − strength.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C33-08 and Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Gaps: product 2, analytics 3, technical 3, behavioral 1. Importance × gap: 6, 9, 6, 3; the total is 24. Hours: 60 × 6 ÷ 24 = 15 (product), 60 × 9 ÷ 24 = 22.5 (analytics), 15 (technical) and 7.5 (behavioral). Analytics gets the most, since it is important and weak; behavioral gets the least, but not zero, because it still needs maintenance. Re-score after a mock and shift hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use a minimum of 5 hours for any round type, so that strengths are maintained, and scale the rest.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A formula guides allocation; judgment adjusts it for rounds that carry veto power.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C33-02 · Write the evidence</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite this answer so that an interviewer can write three evidence statements: &quot;I'd improve the onboarding by making it better for users. I'd test it and see if people like it more.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The scorer's view.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invent a user, a number and a metric and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C33-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;The user I'd focus on is the first-time user, since 44% of new sign-ups leave before finishing onboarding (invented). The main loss is at the permission screen, where 30% stop. I'd test moving the permission request until after the first successful action, measuring onboarding completion as the primary metric, with seven-day retention and permission-grant rate as guardrails; if completion rose by at least three points with no drop in retention, I'd ship it.&quot; Evidence an interviewer could write: <em>chose a specific user with a number; located the problem; defined a primary metric with guardrails and a decision rule.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a risk: &quot;Moving the request may lower the grant rate; I'd watch it.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Specific answers take more thought and leave no doubt.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C33-03 · A company brief</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Build a one-page brief (FW-C33-05) for a company of your choice in 90 minutes, or for the fictional company in Worked example 3 using a different angle. Include your angle and three questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Company preparation.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Public sources.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Ninety minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C33.10 and Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> For the fictional Lumen: <em>Angle:</em> observation, the paywall appears after the third lesson when motivation is highest, which might raise conversion and hurt retention; critique, show the paywall after the first completed streak week and test it, measuring conversion and 90-day retention, with a guardrail on refunds; first thing to examine, conversion by days since sign-up. <em>Questions:</em> How did you choose the three-lesson paywall? What is the retention difference between users who convert early and late? What has the AI partner changed about conversion? Mark the sources as primary or inference.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose a product you use daily and write the angle first, then fill in the facts.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A narrow, well-sourced brief beats a broad shallow one.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C33-04 · Prepare for two loops</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You prepare for two loops. Shared work takes 20 hours; the first company's specific work takes 30 and the second's 25 if done separately. If you prepare each separately, the shared work is repeated. Compute total hours and the saving.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Two processes in one month.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Separately: (20 + 30) + (20 + 25) = 95 hours. Together: 20 + 30 + 25 = 75 hours. Saving: 20 hours, about 21%. The saving is real only if the shared work is done well once. Schedule shared first, then each company's specific work close to its loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If the loops are in different weeks, reuse the first loop's learning to adjust the second plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Sequencing means the second loop gets less recent practice; add a refresher day.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C33-05 · Scope a take-home</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A company sends a case with a 72-hour window and says it should take about six hours: &quot;Should a ride-hailing app launch a scheduled-rides feature?&quot; Write the scope: restatement, time budget, structure, the one number you would compute, and what you would cut.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Non-standard round.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C33-06 and Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Restate:</em> recommend whether to launch scheduled rides, for leadership, in about three pages. <em>Budget:</em> six hours: one to think, two to write, one for numbers, one to edit, one buffer. <em>Structure:</em> page one recommendation and key number; page two users and the problem (who books ahead, such as airport trips, and how many cancel); page three options, the test and risks. <em>The number:</em> the share of rides that are planned (say 8%, invented) times the reliability gain (for example, the drop in failed pickups from 6% to 2% for scheduled rides), and the cost of reserving drivers. <em>Cut:</em> a long competitor survey, secondary features, and anything that does not change the recommendation.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Recommend a limited pilot for airport trips only, with a decision rule on cancellations and driver utilization.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A small scope that is sharp beats broad scope that is thin.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C33-06 · Questions to ask</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write two questions for each of three interviewers: a hiring manager, a peer PM and an engineer.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The end of a round.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C33-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Hiring manager:</em> &quot;What would success look like in the first six months?&quot; &quot;What is the decision the team finds hardest to make right now?&quot; <em>Peer PM:</em> &quot;What is a decision you changed your mind about recently?&quot; &quot;How do you and engineering decide what to cut when a date is at risk?&quot; <em>Engineer:</em> &quot;What makes a PM easy to work with from your side?&quot; &quot;Tell me about a launch that went well and one that didn't, and what the PM did in each.&quot; Follow up on one answer in the next round.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add one question about the team's use of evaluation or experiments if the role involves it.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Two good questions beat five generic ones.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C33-07 · Time-zone and logistics plan</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A loop is scheduled for 9:30 a.m. Pacific time on a day in winter (UTC−8) and you live in India (UTC+5:30). What time is it for you? What if it were summer (UTC−7)? Write a logistics plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Remote interviews.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C33.13.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Winter: 9:30 at UTC−8 is 17:30 UTC, which is 23:00 in India. Summer: 9:30 at UTC−7 is 16:30 UTC, which is 22:00 in India. Plan: confirm the time zone and the local date in writing (the date may differ); sleep earlier the day before and nap in the afternoon; eat early; a quiet room; a charged laptop with power connected, a second internet connection or hotspot; test the camera, audio and meeting link an hour before; keep the recruiter's number to hand; ask whether a different slot is possible if the hour is a problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If rounds run until 3 a.m. local time, ask to split the loop across two days.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Asking for a different time is acceptable if done early and politely.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C33-08 · Write your &quot;why us&quot;</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a 90-second &quot;why this company and why this team&quot; for a company and team of your choice, using the structure in section 4. Mark every claim as primary or inference.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Opening of a loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Your brief.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Section 4 and IQ-C33-01.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> For a fictional company: &quot;I've used Lumen for a month; the streak reminder is stronger than the lesson value after day seven (my observation). In my last role I raised week-two retention by four points by changing a reminder to reflect progress (my result). The team works on the AI conversation partner (primary: the newsroom post), and I'd want to understand how it decides what to remember; my critique is that it doesn't recall past mistakes, which I'd test with a short memory and measure by weekly speaking minutes, with a privacy guardrail. I want to own an area end to end, and a team this size gives that scope. What I'd most like to know is how you measure whether conversation practice leads to retention.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Lead with the problem if the team is known for a specific one.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Specific claims invite probing; that is the point.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C33-09 · Handle a surprise tool</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Two minutes before a product design round, the interviewer says you will use a collaborative whiteboard you have never used. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Non-standard round.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C33.11.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Say so briefly and positively: &quot;I haven't used this tool, so I'll keep my drawings simple.&quot; Spend thirty seconds finding the basics (a text box, a shape, a sticky note). Plan the structure in text first, use the board only for a simple diagram or a table, and narrate what you are drawing. If something fails, switch to speaking and ask the interviewer if you may share notes by typing. Do not let the tool consume time: the interviewer is scoring your thinking.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Ask the interviewer if you may use a document instead.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A simple board leaves more time for thinking.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c33-10\"></span>\n";
